Config Map   stores non-sensitive data / configuration data in key value pair. The Data can later be used for volume mount

Secret  stores  sensitive data such as password / username. It encrypts the data at rest before storing at ETCD 

As User, writes yaml file for config map, kubectl apply to create cm in k8s cluster. 
At the same time API servers saves the info in the etcd. 
Hacker  can get the info of the configap as data is not encrypted. 

Notes about configMaps - 

1- We can Mount ConfigMap as Volume, envVariable, 
2- when a configMap is already in use and we  edit the configMap, it will automatically be updated 
3 - However, ConfigMaps consumed as environment variables are not updated automatically and require a pod restart.

Difference Between Secret and ConfigMap -
Secrets used for sensitive , Data is encrypted at rest. We can enforce strong RBAC for secret 
Config Map stores non-sensitive data / it stores non-sensitive data. ConfigMap data is not encrypted. Hacker  can get the info of the configap as data is not encrypted. 

If data is changing, don’t store cm/secret as environment variable , store it as volume mount 

Secrets are not encrypted , they are encoded. secrets encode data in base64 format. Anyone with the base64 encoded secret can easily decode it. As such the secrets can be considered as not very safe.

Secrets are not encrypted, so it is not safer in that sense. However, some best practices around using secrets make it safer. As in best practices like:
• Not checking-in secret object definition files to source code repositories.
• Enabling Encryption at Rest for Secrets so they are stored encrypted in ETCD. 

Immutable ConfigMaps- 
We cannot change config map values 


The Kubernetes feature Immutable Secrets and ConfigMaps provides an option to set individual Secrets and ConfigMaps as immutable. For clusters that extensively use ConfigMaps (at least tens of thousands of unique ConfigMap to Pod mounts), preventing changes to their data has the following advantages:
	- protects you from accidental (or unwanted) updates that could cause applications outages
	- improves performance of your cluster by significantly reducing load on kube-apiserver, by closing watches for ConfigMaps marked as immutable.
	- Once a ConfigMap is marked as immutable, it is not possible to revert this change nor to mutate the contents of the data or the binaryData field. You can only delete and recreate the ConfigMap. Because existing Pods maintain a mount point to the deleted ConfigMap, it is recommended to recreate these pods.

apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  ...
data:
  ...
immutable: true
